\fs96 \cf2 Lifetimes, origins, and references\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo compiler includes a lifetime checker, a compiler pass that analyzes dataflow through your program. It identifies when variables are valid and inserts destructor calls when a variable's lifetime ends.\
The Mojo compiler uses a special value called an origin to track the lifetime of variables and the validity of references.\
Specifically, an origin answers two questions:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
What variable "owns" this value?\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Can the value be mutated using this reference?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example, consider the following code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 print_str\cf5 (\cf13 s\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 s\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     name\cf5 :\cf13  String \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 "Joan"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     print_str\cf5 (\cf13 name\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 Joan\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The line 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 name = "Joan"
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declares a variable with an identifier (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and logical storage space for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value. When you pass 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  into the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 print_str()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, the function gets an immutable reference to the value. So both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 s
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  refer to the same logical storage space, and have associated origin values that lets the Mojo compiler reason about them.\
Origin tracking and lifetime checking is done at compile time, so origins don't track the actual storage space allocated for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable, for example. Instead, origins track variables symbolically, so the compiler tracks that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 print_str()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is called with a value owned by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the caller's scope. By tracking how owned data flows through the program, the compiler can identify the lifetimes of values.\
Most of the time, origins are handled automatically by the compiler. However, in some cases you'll need to interact with origins directly:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When working with references\'97specifically 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return values.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When working with types like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/pointer/Pointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 Pointer}} or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/span/Span/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 Span}} which are parameterized on the origin of the data they refer to.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This section also covers 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 ref
\fs32 \cb1  arguments\cf2 \ulnone  and 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul ref
\fs32 \cb1  return values\cf2 \ulnone , which let functions take arguments and provide return values as references with parametric origins.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Working with origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo's origin values are mostly created by the compiler, so you can't just create your own origin value\'97you usually need to derive an origin from an existing value.\
Among other things, Mojo uses origins to extend the lifetimes of referenced values, so values aren't destroyed prematurely.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Origin types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo supplies a struct and a set of type aliases (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values) that you can use to specify origin types. As the names suggest, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ImmutOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 MutOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values represent immutable and mutable origins, respectively:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 struct\cf13  \cf14 ImmutRef\cf5 [\cf13 origin\cf5 :\cf13  ImmutOrigin\cf5 ]:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Or you can use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/type_aliases/Origin/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul Origin}} struct to specify an origin with parametric mutability:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 struct\cf13  \cf14 ParametricRef\cf5 [\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     is_mutable\cf5 :\cf13  Bool\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 //,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     origin\cf5 :\cf13  Origin\cf5 [mut=\cf13 is_mutable\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 ]:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Origin types carry the mutability of a reference as a boolean parameter value, indicating whether the origin is mutable, immutable, or even with mutability depending on a parameter specified by the enclosing API.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 is_mutable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter here is an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#infer-only-parameters"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul infer-only parameter}}. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is often inferred, as well. For example, the following code creates a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/pointer/Pointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul Pointer}} to an existing value, but doesn't need to specify an origin\'97the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is inferred from the existing value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 memory \cf5 import\cf13  Pointer\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 use_pointer\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf19 10\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     ptr \cf5 =\cf13  Pointer\cf5 (\cf13 to\cf5 =\cf13 a\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Origin sets\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 An 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 OriginSet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is not a type of origin, it represents a group of origins. Origin sets are used for tracking the lifetimes of values captured in closures.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Origin values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Most origin values are created by the compiler. As a developer, there are a few ways to specify origin values:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Static origin. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 StaticConstantOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value represents immutable values that last for the duration of the program. String literal values have a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 StaticConstantOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Derived origin. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  magic function returns the origin associated with the value (or values) passed in.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Inferred origin. You can use inferred parameters to capture the origin of a value passed in to a function.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
External origins. The external origins, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 MutExternalOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ImmutExternalOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  represent values that are not tracked by the lifetime checker, such as dynamically-allocated memory.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Wildcard origins. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ImmutAnyOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 MutAnyOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values are special cases indicating a reference that might access any live value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Static origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can use the static origin 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 StaticConstantOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when you have a value that exists for the entire duration of the program.\
For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/string_literal/StringLiteral/#as_string_slice"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul as_string_slice()}} returns a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string_slice/StringSlice/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul StringSlice}} pointing to the original string literal. String literals are static\'97they're allocated at compile time and never destroyed\'97so the slice is created with an immutable, static origin.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Derived origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of(value)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator to obtain a value's origin. An argument to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can take an arbitrary expression that yields one of the following:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An origin value.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A value with a memory location.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 origin_of\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12 origin_of\cf5 (\cf13 x\cf5 .\cf13 y\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12 origin_of\cf5 (\cf13 foo\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator is analyzed statically at compile time; The expressions passed to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are never evaluated. (For example, when the compiler analyzes 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of(foo())
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it doesn't run the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 foo()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function.)\
The following struct stores a string value using a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/owned_pointer/OwnedPointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul OwnedPointer}}: a smart pointer that holds an owned value. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 as_ptr()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method returns a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the stored string, using the same origin as the original 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 memory \cf5 import\cf13  OwnedPointer\cf5 ,\cf13  Pointer\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 struct\cf13  \cf14 BoxedString\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  o_ptr\cf5 :\cf13  OwnedPointer\cf5 [\cf13 String\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf14 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  value\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         self\cf5 .\cf13 o_ptr \cf5 =\cf13  OwnedPointer\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf14 as_ptr\cf5 (mut\cf13  self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Pointer\cf5 [\cf13 String\cf5 ,\cf13  origin_of\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 .\cf13 o_ptr\cf5 )]:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf5 return\cf13  Pointer\cf5 (\cf13 to\cf5 =\cf13 self\cf5 .\cf13 o_ptr\cf5 [])\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 as_ptr()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method takes its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . If it used the default 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument convention, it would be immutable, and the derived origin (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of(self.o_ptr)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) would also be immutable.\
You can also pass multiple expressions to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to express the union of two or more origins:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of(a, b)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Origin unions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The union of two or more origins creates a new origin that references all of the original origins for the purposes of lifetime extension (so a union of the origins of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 b
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  extends both lifetimes).\
An origin union is mutable if and only if all of its constituent origins are mutable. For an example, see \cf5 \ul Return values with union origins\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Inferred origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Since origins are parameters, the compiler can infer an origin value from an the argument passed to a function or method, as described in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#parameter-inference"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Parameter inference}}. This allows a function to return a value that has the same origin as the argument passed to it.\
See the section on 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul ref
\fs32 \cb1  arguments\cf2 \ulnone  for an example using an inferred origin.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 External origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The external origins, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 MutExternalOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ImmutExternalOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  represent values that do not alias any existing value. That is, they point to memory that is not owned by any other variable, and are therefore not tracked by the lifetime checker. For example, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/alloc/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul alloc()}} function returns an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a new dynamically-allocated block of memory, with the origin 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 MutExternalOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The origin indicates that the memory is not managed by the Mojo ownership system. When you use an unsafe API like this, you're responsible for managing the lifetime yourself: for example, a struct that allocates memory should generally free that memory in its destructor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Wildcard origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The wildcard origins, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ImmutAnyOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 MutAnyOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , are special cases indicating a reference that might access any live value. These were previously widely used for unsafe pointers. Using a pointer with a wildcard origin into a scope effectively disables Mojo's ASAP destruction for any values in that scope, as long as the pointer is live. Accordingly, the use of wildcard origins is discouraged, and should be used as a last resort.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Working with references\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword with arguments and return values to specify a reference with parametric mutability. That is, they can be either mutable or immutable.\
From inside the called function, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument looks like a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument.\
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return value looks like any other return value to the calling function, but it is a reference to an existing value, not a copy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument convention lets you specify an argument of parametric mutability: that is, you don't need to know in advance whether the passed argument will be mutable or immutable. There are several reasons you might want to use a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You want to accept an argument with parametric mutability.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You want to tie the lifetime of one argument to the lifetime of another argument.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When you want an argument that is guaranteed to be passed in memory: this can be important and useful for generic arguments that need an identity, irrespective of whether the concrete type is register passable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The syntax for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref arg_name: arg_type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
Or:\

\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref[origin_specifier(s)] arg_name: arg_type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
In the first form, the origin and mutability of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is inferred from the value passed in. The second form includes an origin clause, consisting of one or more origin specifiers inside square brackets. An origin specifier can be either:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An origin value.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An arbitrary expression, which is treated as shorthand for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin_of(expression)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . In other words, the following declarations are equivalent:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 ref[\cf13 origin_of\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 )]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
ref[\cf13 self\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf13 \cb12 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/pointer/AddressSpace/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul AddressSpace}} value.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An underscore character (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 _
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to indicate that the origin is unbound. This is equivalent to omitting the origin specifier.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 add_ref\cf5 (ref\cf13  a\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13  b\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf13 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf5 return\cf13  a\cf5 +\cf13 b\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf13 \cb12 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also name the origin explicitly. This is useful if you want to restrict the argument to either a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ImmutOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 MutOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or if you want to bind a function's return value to the origin of an argument.\
For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Span
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type is a non-owning view of contiguous data (like a substring of a string, or a subset of a list). Because it points to data that it doesn't own, it is parameterized on an origin value that represents the lifetime and ownership of the data it points to.\
In the following example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 to_byte_span()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 List[Byte]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and returns a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Span[Byte]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with the same origin as the list:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 collections \cf5 import\cf13  List\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 memory \cf5 import\cf13  Span\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  to_byte_span\cf5 [\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     is_mutable\cf5 :\cf13  Bool\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 //,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     origin\cf5 :\cf13  Origin\cf5 [mut=\cf13 is_mutable\cf5 ],\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 ](ref[\cf13 origin\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Byte\cf5 ])\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Span\cf5 [\cf13 Byte\cf5 ,\cf13  origin\cf5 ]:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 return\cf13  Span\cf5 (\cf14 list\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf14 list\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Byte\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf19 77\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf19 111\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf19 106\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf19 111\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     span \cf5 =\cf13  to_byte_span\cf5 (\cf14 list\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter is inferred from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 list
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, and then used as the origin for the returned 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Span
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Since the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Span
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  takes on the origin of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 list
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, the Mojo compiler can identify the span's data as owned by the list. The span will have the same lifetime as the list, and the span will be mutable if the list is mutable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  return values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return values allow a function to return a mutable or immutable reference to a value. The syntax for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return value is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 -> ref[origin_specifier(s)] arg_type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
Note that you must provide an origin specifier for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return value. The values allowed for origin specifiers are the same as the ones listed for 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul ref
\fs32 \cb1  arguments\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return values can be an efficient way to handle updating items in a collection. The standard way to do this is by implementing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dunder methods. These are invoked to read from and write to a subscripted item in a collection:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 value \cf5 =\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 [\cf13 a\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf14 \cb12 list\cf5 [\cf13 b\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf19 10\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 With a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can return a mutable reference that can be modified directly. This has pros and cons compared to using a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The mutable reference is more efficient\'97a single update isn't broken up across two methods. However, the referenced value must be in memory.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  pair allows for arbitrary code to be run when values are retrieved and set. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can validate or constrain input values.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example, in the following example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 NameList
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that returns a reference:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 struct\cf13  \cf14 NameList\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  names\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 String\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf14 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 *\cf13 names\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         self\cf5 .\cf13 names \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 []\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf5 for\cf13  name \cf5 in\cf13  names\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12             self\cf5 .\cf13 names\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 name\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf14 __getitem__\cf5 (ref\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  index\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  \cf5 ref[\cf13 self\cf5 .\cf13 names\cf5 ]\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf5 if\cf13  \cf5 (\cf13 index \cf5 >=\cf19 0\cf13  \cf5 and\cf13  index \cf5 <\cf13  \cf14 len\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 .\cf13 names\cf5 )):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12             \cf5 return\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 names\cf5 [\cf13 index\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf5 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12             \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf15 "index out of bounds"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf14 list\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  NameList\cf5 (\cf15 "Thor"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "Athena"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "Dana"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "Vrinda"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 ref\cf13  name \cf5 =\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 [\cf19 2\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 name\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     name \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf15 "?"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf14 list\cf5 [\cf19 2\cf5 ])\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 Dana\cb1 \
\cb12 Dana?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Note the use of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax to create a reference binding.\
If you assign a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return value to a variable, the variable receives a copy of the referenced item. Use a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/variables/#reference-bindings"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul reference binding}} if you need to capture the reference for future use:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 var\cf13  name_copy \cf5 =\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 [\cf19 2\cf5 ]\cf13   \cf6 # owned copy of list[2]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 ref\cf13  name_ref \cf5 =\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 [\cf19 2\cf5 ]\cf13   \cf6 # reference to list[2]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Parametric mutability of return values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Another advantage of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return arguments is the ability to support parametric mutability. For example, recall the signature of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method above:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 __getitem__\cf5 (ref\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  index\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  \cf5 ref[\cf13 self\cf5 ]\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Since the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  of the return value is tied to the origin of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the returned reference will be mutable if the method was called using a mutable reference. The method still works if you have an immutable reference to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 NameList
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but it returns an immutable reference:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 pass_immutable_list\cf5 (\cf14 list\cf5 :\cf13  NameList\cf5 )\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf14 list\cf5 [\cf19 2\cf5 ])\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf6 # list[2] += "?" # Error, this list is immutable\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf14 list\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  NameList\cf5 (\cf15 "Sophie"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "Jack"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "Diana"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     pass_immutable_list\cf5 (\cf14 list\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 Diana\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Without parametric mutability, you'd need to write two versions of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , one that accepts an immutable 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and another that accepts a mutable 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Return values with union origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return value can include multiple values in its origin specifier, which yields the union of the origins. For example, the following 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 pick_one()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function returns a reference to one of the two input strings, with an origin that's a union of both origins.\
